Z||000000||Haze_Performance||02-06-2001||02:36 AM||||OK..Before everyone Flames me, Hear me out... I have an idea.<P>I looked into getting replacement Speedo, Radio & Shifter panels made out of Billet Alum.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <B><I>WAY TO MUCH $$$</I></B>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  There was also the problem with the thickness of the Billet panels. It would stick up a little to high on the shifter panel.<P>So how about Chrome plating the stock panels? <P>I put a Billet wheel & installation kit in my '85. I was also thinking about chrome plating the top part on the column to match the wheel & kit.<P>Anyone chrome plate??  Im willing to donate some good panels to the cause   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>WS6 Fieros "Members Photo Page"</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 02-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||02-06-2001||09:29 AM||||I think you need some metal base to do chrome plating.  The part needs to conduct electric current.<P>Nolan Concepts does chrome plating.  I don't have his web page, but he comes to many of the shows.||||reg||3
Z||000002||Oreif||02-06-2001||10:12 AM||||I saw a dash in a Corvette once that the guy took a sheet of stainless steel had a shop plasma cut-out the shapes then he polished the steel and glued it on top of the original dash pieces and then sprayed a glossy clearcoat over it. It looked like Chrome plating. Or you could use 600 grit and Brush it in one direction to make it look like brushed aluminum. Just a thought.<BR>Plastic is dipped into a special plating to chrome it. Sort of how they do for models. I have no idea who could do it. Maybe Revell/Monogram models in Des Plaines?||||reg||1
